{
 "id": "M22",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "A handful of these backdoors alarm Opus and nothing else",
 "section": "results",
 "order": 135,
 "caption": "One row per hard backdoor, 51 of them, ordered by what Opus 5.5 gave it at xhigh effort. All three monitors are on a 0 to 100 scale. Opus clears 50 on 13 rows where Sonnet and Haiku never go above 30. Below that the three agree that nothing is wrong.",
 "notes": [
  "A filled Opus mark is a backdoor no monitor caught at all. A hollow one is a backdoor some panel monitor did catch, which is why it is in this set but not in the never-caught group.",
  "Opus has no honest baseline here, so it has no cutoff of its own and no audit budget. Its scores say what it thinks of a program, not what it would have audited. Treat 50 as a reading aid, not an operating point.",
  "The ordering is Opus's own, so the chart cannot help but look favourable to Opus at the top. The comparison that matters is horizontal, within a row, where Sonnet and Haiku had the same code in front of them.",
  "Sonnet's and Haiku's medians on this set are 1 and 2 out of 100, which is what being a hard backdoor means: these are the programs the panel found boring.",
  "Thin data. 51 rows is the whole hard set, and the never-caught group inside it is 41. Single scores, one pass each, no repeat draws, so a row's height is one measurement."
 ],
